content.text:
Done. Word-boundary truncation, hard cut as fallback.

**`format.js`** — one block after `suffix`, so `maxLength` bounds the final output:

```js
if (options.maxLength !== undefined && result.length > options.maxLength) {
  const ellipsis = "...";
  const budget = options.maxLength - ellipsis.length;

  if (budget <= 0) {
    return ellipsis.slice(0, options.maxLength);
  }

  const boundary = result.slice(0, budget + 1).lastIndexOf(" ");
  const cut = boundary === -1 ? budget : boundary;
  result = result.slice(0, cut).trimEnd() + ellipsis;
}
```

One correction to what I described before implementing: I said "find the last space in the slice," which is subtly wrong. Searching `slice(0, budget)` means a cut landing *exactly* at a word end still backs off to the previous word — `format("the quick brown fox", {maxLength: 12})` would give `"the..."` instead of `"the quick..."`. Searching `budget + 1` treats a space sitting right at the boundary as the clean break. The test at `format.test.js:15` is the one that caught it.

**`format.test.js`** — 12 new assertions in the existing `console.assert` style: untouched-when-short, exact boundary, word-boundary cut, whole-words-to-budget, mid-word backoff, no-boundary hard fallback, whitespace trim, `maxLength` of 3 / 2 / 0, composition with `prefix`, composition with `uppercase`.

Verification: ran `node format.test.js` before implementing — 10 of the 12 failed, each because the feature was missing (the two "untouched" cases correctly passed from the start). After implementing, all pass with no assertion output.

Two notes:
- `node` emits a pre-existing `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning on every run because `package.json` has no `"type": "module"`. Unrelated to this change; left alone.
- The existing harness prints "All tests passed" unconditionally — `console.assert` logs but doesn't set a non-zero exit. A failing assertion is visible in the output but wouldn't fail CI. Pre-existing, out of scope, worth fixing if this project ever grows a CI step.

Not committed.
